<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Pretraining with <span id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment setup. </span>We evaluate the proposed <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>framework by pre-training LLaMA-2 models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> with size varying from 60M to 7B. Following the existing experimental settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, we train the model over C4-en dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, which is primarily intended for pre-training language models and word representations with large scale. We compared <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>with existing parameter-efficient methods including LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, ReLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, SLTrain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, CoLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">with aligned parameter complexity</span>. We also compared <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>with other optimizer-efficient algorithms including GaLore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, RSO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and Apollo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">with aligned memory overhead</span>. We use the same configurations as those reported in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, and the detailed experiment setting are in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2.SSS1" title="D.2.1 Experimental set up ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-training results. </span>Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> has shown that <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>generally outperforms other parameter-efficient with the same of lower training parameters. It can be observed that <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>even achieve a better performance as full-rank training while reducing the parameter complexity by 56.5% and the per-step computation complexity by 63.2% for LLaMA-2 1B network.
When aligning the memory overhead, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>achieves a better validation perplexity than that of optimizer-efficient algorithms especially with the model size larger than 1B, showing the benefits of <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>with large-scale scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the through of ours when pretraining LLaMA-2 1B model on a A100 40G GPU with batch size 16, from which one can observe that <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>achieve the best throughout compared with other methods. Even when considering the data-parallel communication overhead across 4 GPUs, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>achieves over 6% improvement compared to state-of-the-art methods without such communication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the validation perplexity of <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>when training with LLaMA2-7B as well as other competitive approaches. It can be observed that <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>outperforms all baselines in the training process with a lower memory overhead when using re-computation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S5.F6.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The throughout of algorithms for training LLaMA-2 1B on a Nvidia A100 40G GPU. The result of other comparable methods are from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption><img src="2509.18993v1/throughout.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/168;" width="224" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S5.F6.fig4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The evaluation perplexity for <span id="S5.F6.fig4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>in training LLaMA-2 350M model with different strategies of rank selection.</span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18993v1/ablation1.svg" id="S5.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/154;" width="224" height="154"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F6.fig6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The comparsion of evaluation perplexity for <span id="S5.F6.fig6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>in training LLaMA-2 350M with fixed <math id="S5.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and learnable <math id="S5.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18993v1/ablation3_350m.svg" id="S5.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/154;" width="224" height="154"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig8" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S5.F6.fig8.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The evaluation perplexity for <span id="S5.F6.fig8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span>, Learnable-ResFormer and DenseFormer in the LLaMA-2 1B pre-training task.</span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18993v1/ablation2_new.svg" id="S5.F6.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/154;" width="224" height="154"></object>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Ablations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How does rank selection impact pre-training performance? </span> We train the LLaMA-2 350B network with <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>using identical parameters but varying ranks across transformer layers. Experimental details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2.SSS5" title="D.2.5 Experimental setting and additional results for the ablations ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2.5</span></a>. Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that networks with higher ranks in middle layers and lower ranks in side layers exhibit superior performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Whether does the learnable scaling factor <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> benefit the model convergence? </span> We train the LLaMA-2 350M network with the same hyperparameters as that in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> except make the scaling <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. From the perplexity shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we can obtain that training <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>with a learnable <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> can improve the numerical stability as there are spikes in the runs with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed. This match our ovservation in Section that the best <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve the lowest rank varies during the training process. More discussions can be found in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title='E.2 Additional discussion for the learnable scaling factor 𝛽_𝑙^"P". ‣ Appendix E Additional discussions ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Whether other cross-layer residual strategies do well in pre-training with low-rank parameters? </span>We train the LLaMA-2 1B network with low-rank parameter for 50000 iterations in all linear layers except the first transformer layer with <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>, Learnable-ResFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> and DenseFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the evaluation perplexity of both cross-layer residual architectures. It can be observation that <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>outperforms the ResFormer architecture in pre-training with low-rank parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span>, a parameter-efficient LLM pretraining framework that computes linear activations via low-rank transformations with cross-layer residuals. This dual-path design preserves high-rank capacity using fewer parameters, achieving better validation performance with reduced computation and memory than conventional low-rank frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We outline two directions for future work on <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>framework. First, system-level implementations integrating mixed-precision training could alleviate the growing memory overhead encountered when scaling <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> to larger models, particularly for activation recomputation strategies. Second, generalizing the framework’s current multi-head attention foundation to alternative attention architectures, including multi-head latent attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, would broaden its architectural versatility.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
