</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">D.2.3 </span>Scaling <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>to a larger model size</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the performance of <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> with a larger model size, we pre-train a LLaMA-2 13B model under 8-bit Adam and <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> separately. We use the re-computation present in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 CR-Net with activation-efficient re-compuation ‣ 3 CR-Net: Cross-layer residual network for LLM pre-training ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> for <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> and save a series of full activation every 8 layers. We trained 40K steps due to the time limitation. The hyperparameter <math id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> is set to 1260. The evaluation perplexity shown as Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ D.2.2 CR-Net accelerates the pre-training tasks ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> illustrates the performance of <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> in 13B model, which demonstrates that <span id="A4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> exhibits a comparable performance than full-rank training with 8-bit Adam. Thus can be scaled to models with 13B parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">D.2.4 </span>Downstream performance</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine the downstream performance of our proposed framework, we fine-tune LLaMA-2 1B models pre-trained by GaLore and <span id="A4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> for 15.6B tokens. The training set up is the same as that in the manuscript. The fine-tune datasets consist of Wikitext-2 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> and 30K arXiv abstracts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. We tune each data for 8 epochs. Table <a href="#A4.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ D.2.2 CR-Net accelerates the pre-training tasks ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> presents the evaluation perplexity and accuracy of these tasks, which illustrates that <span id="A4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> has better performance than GaLore in these downstream tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">D.2.5 </span>Experimental setting and additional results for the ablations</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the experimental setting and additional results for the ablations we presented in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Ablations ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How does rank selection impact pre-training performance? </span>In this ablation, we train LLaMA-2 350M with <span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>for 60K iterations. The max learning rate is set to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and all the other configs is the same as that in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2.SSS1" title="D.2.1 Experimental set up ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2.1</span></a>. For the selection of layer ranks, we present the following three strategies to ensure the their parameters are all the same:</p>
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1: <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=256</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Layer 2-8. <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=192</annotation></semantics></math> in Layer 9-24.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S2: <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=256</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Layer 10-16. <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=192</annotation></semantics></math> in Layer 2-9 and Layer 17-24.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S3: <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=256</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Layer 18-24. <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=192</annotation></semantics></math> in Layer 2-17.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Whether does the learnable scale factor <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> benefit the model convergence? </span>In this ablation, we use <span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>to train LLaMA-2 350M and LLaMA-2 1B model with a learnable <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a series of fixed <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=2,3,\cdots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=2,3,\cdots,L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{P}\in\{\text{Q},\text{K},\text{V},\text{O},\text{gate},\text{up},\text{down}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>P</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>K</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>V</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>O</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>gate</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>up</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>down</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{P}\in\{\text{Q},\text{K},\text{V},\text{O},\text{gate},\text{up},\text{down}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The fixed <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> varies from <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1,0.2,0.5,1.0,2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1,0.2,0.5,1.0,2.0</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaMA-2 350M and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaMA-2 1B. We trained 40000 iterations for LLaMA-2 350M and 80000 iterations for LLaMA-2 1B, respectively. Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ D.2.5 Experimental setting and additional results for the ablations ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrates the evaluation perplexity of each cases, shown the benefit of letting <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> learnable as the higher validation performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F9.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18993v1/x1.svg" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/148;" width="215" height="148"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F9.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.18993v1/ablation3.svg" id="A4.F9.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/148;" width="215" height="148"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>The comparsion of evaluation perplexity for <span id="A4.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>in pre-training tasks with fixed <math id="A4.F9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> and learnable <math id="A4.F9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mi>l</mi><mtext>P</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{l}^{\text{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Left: LLaMA-2 350M, right: LLaMA-2 1B.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Whether other cross-layer residual strategies do well in pre-training with low-rank parameters? </span>To compare <span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>with different efficient cross-layer residual strategies for LLMs pre-training including ResFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> and DenseFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, we pre-trained a LLaMA-2 1B model with <span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span>, Learnable-ResFormer, and DenseFormer using low-rank parameters. The rank <math id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> for transformer layers (except the first layer) is set to 448, while all other configs are the same as those in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2.SSS1" title="D.2.1 Experimental set up ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2.1</span></a>. We stopped training early at the 50,000-th step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the evaluation perplexity of <span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>and other cross-layer residual strategies for the LLaMA-2 1B training task. It can be observed that <span id="A4.SS2.SSS5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>outperforms the ResFormer architecture and DenseFormer in pre-training with low-rank parameters.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Comparsion of validation perplexity (<math id="A4.T12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) of different approaches in LLaMA-2 pre-training tasks with longer sequence length and more training tokens.</figcaption>
