</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Pre-training GQA and MoE models with <span id="A4.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span></h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present additional experiments on pre-training the LLaMA-3 model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> with a grouped query attention (GQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> architecture, as well as pre-training a Qwen-3-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> model with a mixture of experts (MoE) architecture, to validate the proposed framework across different model structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-training LLaMA-3 with GQA architecture. </span>We pre-trained a 1B-parameter LLaMA-3 model on 15.6B tokens from the C4-en dataset using both GaLore and <span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> methods. The model hyperparameters are shown in Table <a href="#A4.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ D.2.6 Pre-training with longer sequence and more training tokens ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. The low-rank coefficient rr was set to 512 for GaLore and 448 for <span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span>. The remaining configuration follows the same setup as the LLaMA-2 pre-training task described in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>. The evaluation perplexity results are presented in Table <a href="#A4.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ D.2.6 Pre-training with longer sequence and more training tokens ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, indicating that <span id="A4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> outperforms GaLore in pre-training tasks on the LLaMA-3 model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-training models with MoE architecture. </span>We pre-trained a Qwen-3-based model under the MoE architecture, containing 1.8B total parameters with 650M activated parameters, on 7.8B tokens from the C4-en dataset. Training was conducted under both full-rank and <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> settings. The model hyperparameters are provided in Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.1 Experimental details for the observation of cross-layer low-rank structure ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The low-rank coefficient rr in <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> was set to 224. For <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span>, we replaced the parameter matrices within the linear operators of the MoE layers. Each MoE layer was treated as a unified operator, and cross-layer residual connections were applied after obtaining the output of the MoE operators. The remaining experimental setup matches that of the LLaMA-2 pre-training task in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>. The evaluation perplexity, shown in Table <a href="#A4.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ D.2.6 Pre-training with longer sequence and more training tokens ‣ D.2 Experimental details for C4 pre-training tasks ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, demonstrates the competitive performance of <span id="A4.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net</span> compared to full-rank training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional discussions</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Per-Step Computation Acceleration Analysis.</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the computation efficiency of <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>in practical training tasks, we provide the computation complexity of different efficient pre-training approaches for one gradient step (based on LLaMA-2 architecture) in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> as Table <a href="#A5.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ E.1 Per-Step Computation Acceleration Analysis. ‣ Appendix E Additional discussions ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>. It can be observed that <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>achieves the same training performance as full-rank training with <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>36.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> computation overhead for both LLaMA-2 1B and 7B models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T16" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 16: </span>Computation complexity of different efficient pre-training approaches for one gradient step based on LLaMA architecture for the task in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Pretraining with CR-Net ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Lower-order terms are omitted for brevity. The selection of the rank <math id="A5.T16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="A5.T16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CR-Net </span>are based on Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ D.1 Experimental details for the observation of cross-layer low-rank structure ‣ Appendix D Experimental details and additional experiments ‣ CR-Net: Scaling Parameter-Efficient Training with Cross-Layer Low-Rank Structure" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> while that of the other algorithms are based on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
